---
/**
 * The seam between two sections, stated rather than left to whitespace alone. Short and
 * centred rather than full-bleed — a line that stops well short of both edges reads as
 * a deliberate mark, where one running edge to edge reads as a rule the browser drew for
 * free. The mark at its centre is the same wordless glyph used everywhere else the brand
 * appears alone, so this reads as the site's own seam, not a generic `<hr>`.
 *
 * This is also the *only* source of vertical space between two story sections — every
 * section that sits next to a divider carries no padding-block of its own for that
 * side. One number, defined once, used by both neighbours automatically: the gap above
 * a divider and the gap below it can't drift apart the way they could if each section
 * were setting its own top/bottom padding independently, which is exactly how they'd
 * gone uneven before this.
 */
---

<div class="divider" role="presentation">
  <span class="divider__line"></span>
  <img class="divider__mark" src="/icon.svg" alt="" width="32" height="32" />
  <span class="divider__line"></span>
</div>

<style>
  .divider {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-sm);
    width: min(100%, 18rem);
    margin-block: var(--space-2xl);
    margin-inline: auto;
  }

  /* Half the desktop gap, not a nudge off it. A phone's viewport is roughly a third
     the height of a laptop's, so a 4rem gap eats a far bigger share of the screen
     there and reads as a hole rather than a breath. */
  @media (width < 56rem) {
    .divider {
      margin-block: var(--space-lg);
    }
  }

  .divider__line {
    flex: 1;
    height: 1px;
    background-color: var(--color-line);
  }

  .divider__mark {
    width: 1.1rem;
    height: 1.1rem;
    opacity: 0.55;
  }
</style>
